      <!-- PROJECT GOAL -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Project Goal</p>

        <h2>
          Generating a high-voltage electric field through resonant oscillation.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The goal was to build a self-oscillating Slayer Exciter capable of
          producing a high-frequency, high-voltage electric field. The field
          could induce current in nearby fluorescent lamps, allowing them to
          illuminate without a direct wired electrical connection.
        </p>

        <p>
          The circuit uses feedback between the primary and secondary coils to
          repeatedly switch a transistor on and off. Energy transfers between
          the secondary-coil inductance and its effective parasitic capacitance,
          producing oscillation near the secondary resonant frequency.
        </p>

        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div class="concept-image">
            <img
              src="/projects/slayer-exciter/slayer-exciter-concept.png"
              alt="Slayer Exciter operating concept circuit"
            />
          </div>

          <div class="operation-steps">
            <article>
              <span>1</span>
              <strong>Transistor Turns On</strong>
              <p>Primary-coil current builds and creates a magnetic field.</p>
            </article>

            <article>
              <span>2</span>
              <strong>Secondary Feedback</strong>
              <p>Mutual inductance creates voltage across the secondary coil.</p>
            </article>

            <article>
              <span>3</span>
              <strong>Transistor Turns Off</strong>
              <p>Negative feedback at the transistor base interrupts primary current.</p>
            </article>

            <article>
              <span>4</span>
              <strong>LC Oscillation</strong>
              <p>Energy oscillates between secondary inductance and parasitic capacitance.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- COIL CHARACTERIZATION -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Coil Design & Characterization</p>

        <h2>
          Measuring the transformer before modeling the circuit.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The primary and secondary coils were wound experimentally and then
          measured with an LCR meter. These measured inductances were used in
          the circuit model and helped explain how changes in coil geometry
          affected resonant frequency and output strength.
        </p>

        <div class="parameter-grid">
          <article>
            <span>Initial Secondary</span>
            <strong>≈ 670 turns</strong>
            <p>≈ 1.6 mH</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Final Secondary</span>
            <strong>≈ 800 turns</strong>
            <p>1.7–1.9 mH</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Final Primary</span>
            <strong>8–10 turns</strong>
            <p>1.3–1.5 µH</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="two-image-grid">
          <div>
            <div class="lcr-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/slayer-exciter/primary-coil-lcr.jpeg"
                alt="Primary coil inductance measurement with LCR meter"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Primary-coil inductance measurement.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <div class="lcr-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/slayer-exciter/secondary-coil-lcr.jpeg"
                alt="Secondary coil inductance measurement with LCR meter"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Secondary-coil inductance measurement used for the resonant model.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- INITIAL PROTOTYPE -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Initial Prototype</p>

        <h2>
          Building a working circuit and comparing it with simulation.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The first prototype used a 2N2222A transistor, 47 kΩ base resistor,
          1N4148 diode, approximately 1.27 µH primary coil, and approximately
          1.6 mH secondary coil. The measured coil values were incorporated
          into a SIMetrix model before testing the physical circuit.
        </p>

        <div class="two-image-grid">
          <div>
            <div class="schematic-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/slayer-exciter/initial-simulation-schematic.png"
                alt="Initial Slayer Exciter SIMetrix schematic"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Version 1 SIMetrix model using measured coil inductances.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <div class="scope-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/slayer-exciter/initial-base-oscillation.png"
                alt="Measured initial transistor base oscillation"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Initial physical base-voltage measurement showing approximately
              1.85 MHz oscillation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="result-cards">
          <article>
            <span>Measured Frequency</span>
            <strong>1.85 MHz</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Period</span>
            <strong>539 ns</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Base Voltage</span>
            <strong>1.12 Vpp</strong>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- FAILURE ANALYSIS -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Failure Analysis</p>

        <h2>
          The first prototype worked, but the transistor did not survive continuous operation.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The initial circuit successfully generated a usable electric field,
          but the 2N2222A repeatedly overheated and failed. Oscilloscope
          measurements revealed that the transistor base could be pulled to
          approximately −10 V, substantially more negative than predicted by the
          simulation.
        </p>

        <p>
          This measurement redirected the design effort toward the protection
          diode and transistor selection rather than simply increasing supply
          voltage or coil size.
        </p>

        <div class="failure-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/slayer-exciter/base-voltage-failure.png"
            alt="Measured transistor base waveform showing excessive negative voltage"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="figure-caption">
          Measured base-voltage excursion that exposed a transistor reliability
          problem in the first design.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- COMPONENT OPTIMIZATION -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Component Optimization</p>

        <h2>
          Selecting components for both switching speed and electrical durability.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Replacing the 1N4148 with a 1N4001 reduced the magnitude of the
          negative base-voltage excursion and lowered supply current from roughly
          0.4 A to approximately 40 mA without a meaningful reduction in the
          observed field strength.
        </p>

        <div class="highlight-result">
          <span>Diode Change</span>
          <strong>≈10× lower supply current</strong>
          <p>~0.4 A → ~40 mA</p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Transistor testing showed that the final device needed both high
          electrical durability and enough switching speed to operate near the
          resonant frequency. Several BJT transistor types including the 2N2222A & TTD1409B were evaluated before the
          TIP31C was selected for the final design.
        </p>

        <div class="tradeoff-grid">
          <article>
            <span>2N2222A</span>
            <strong>Fast enough</strong>
            <p>Worked initially, but repeatedly overheated and failed.</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>TTD1409B</span>
            <strong>Durable but too slow</strong>
            <p>Could not switch quickly enough to sustain oscillation.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="selected-card">
            <span>TIP31C</span>
            <strong>Final selection</strong>
            <p>Combined adequate switching speed with substantially better durability.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- COIL OPTIMIZATION -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Resonant Structure</p>

        <h2>
          Increasing inductance and refining the topload.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Later versions used more secondary turns and slightly larger primary
          inductance. The final secondary used roughly 800 turns and measured
          between 1.7 and 1.9 mH. A larger spherical aluminum-foil topload
          increased effective capacitance and helped lower the resonant frequency.
        </p>

        <p>
          Increased output voltage also caused arcing between the primary and
          secondary coils. Additional insulation was added between the windings
          to prevent breakdown and allow higher-energy operation.
        </p>

        <div class="coil-assembly-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/slayer-exciter/final-coil-assembly.jpeg"
            alt="Final Slayer Exciter primary coil, secondary coil, and spherical topload"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="figure-caption">
          Final resonant structure with primary coil, high-turn secondary coil,
          and spherical topload.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- FINAL DESIGN -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Final Design</p>

        <h2>
          A stronger and more durable Slayer Exciter.
        </h2>

        <div class="final-spec-grid">
          <article>
            <span>Supply</span>
            <strong>16–21 V</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Transistor</span>
            <strong>TIP31C</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Protection Diode</span>
            <strong>1N4001</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Base Resistor</span>
            <strong>47 kΩ</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Primary</span>
            <strong>1.3–1.5 µH</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Secondary</span>
            <strong>1.7–1.9 mH</strong>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="final-waveform-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/slayer-exciter/final-base-oscillation.png"
            alt="Final TIP31C transistor base-voltage waveform"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="figure-caption">
          Final TIP31C base-voltage waveform oscillating at approximately
          1.05 MHz.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- HIGH VOLTAGE MEASUREMENT -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">High-Voltage Measurement</p>

        <h2>
          Measuring an output beyond the normal oscilloscope range.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Direct output measurement was difficult because the Slayer Exciter
          exceeded the available oscilloscope range. To increase attenuation,
          multiple 10× probes were cascaded to create higher-ratio measurement
          probes.
        </p>

        <div class="measurement-results">
          <article>
            <span>Oscillation</span>
            <strong>≈ 1.05 MHz</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Captured Output</span>
            <strong>&gt; 386 V</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Estimated Output</span>
            <strong>≈ 1.5–2 kV</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Effective Capacitance</span>
            <strong>≈ 12.4 pF</strong>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="measurement-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/slayer-exciter/high-voltage-measurement.png"
            alt="High-voltage Slayer Exciter output measurement setup"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="figure-caption">
          High-voltage measurement setup using cascaded oscilloscope probes to
          increase attenuation.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- FINAL RESULT -->
      <section class="content-section result-section">
        <p class="section-label">Final Result</p>

        <h2>
          Lighting fluorescent lamps without a wired electrical connection.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The final design produced a visibly stronger electric field than the
          first prototype while avoiding the repeated transistor failures. The
          usable fluorescent-lamp illumination range increased from roughly
          6–12 inches to approximately 12–20 inches.
        </p>

        <div class="wireless-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/slayer-exciter/wireless-lighting.jpeg"
            alt="Fluorescent lamp illuminated wirelessly by the Slayer Exciter"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="figure-caption">
          Wireless fluorescent-lamp illumination produced by the final Slayer
          Exciter.
        </p>

        <div class="final-result">
          <span>Final Result</span>
          <strong>
            Stable high-frequency oscillation, improved field strength, and
            wireless fluorescent-lamp illumination at approximately 12–20 inches.
          </strong>
        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- FIELD EFFECTS -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Field Effects</p>

        <h2>
          Observing high-field behavior beyond wireless lighting.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The final Slayer Exciter also produced visible arcing and measurable
          electromagnetic interference in nearby electronics, demonstrating how
          strongly the high-frequency field coupled into surrounding conductors
          and capacitive sensing systems.
        </p>

        <div class="arcing-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/slayer-exciter/arcing-demonstration.jpeg"
            alt="Visible arc produced by the Slayer Exciter"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="figure-caption">
          Visible arcing produced by the high-voltage oscillating field.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- WHAT I LEARNED -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">What I Learned</p>

        <h2>
          Connecting resonance, component limits, and real electromagnetic behavior.
        </h2>

        <p>
          This project showed how strongly real component behavior can affect a
          resonant circuit. Measured coil inductance established the model,
          oscilloscope measurements exposed transistor stress that was not fully
          captured by simulation, and repeated component testing led to a more
          durable final design.
        </p>

        <div class="skills">
          <span>Resonant Circuits</span>
          <span>LC Resonance</span>
          <span>High-Frequency Switching</span>
          <span>BJT Selection</span>
          <span>Inductor Design</span>
          <span>LCR Measurement</span>
          <span>Oscilloscope Testing</span>
          <span>SIMetrix</span>
          <span>Electromagnetic Fields</span>
          <span>High-Voltage Measurement</span>
          <span>Iterative Hardware Design</span>
        </div>
      </section>


